20:32:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you. That sentence—*"nothing, anywhere, ever has a starting point"*—sounds completely absurd on the face of it, and you're right to call it out. The Big Bang was indeed the start of everything, so it feels not just natural but mandatory to ask: "Okay, *where* did that start happen?" Let me gently push back on just one word in your summary: **"where."** The entire puzzle hinges on the difference between a *when* and a *where*. The Big Bang has a "when" (about 13.8 billion years ago), but it doesn't have a "where" in the way you're imagining—and that distinction is what reconciles your very reasonable demand for a start with the no-centre model. ### The Big Bang is the start of *time*, not an event at a place in space In our daily lives, every beginning is an event that happens at a specific location at a specific time. The start of a race has a gun go off at the start line. Your birth had a date and a hospital. So naturally, you think the universe's beginning must also be an event with a date and an address. But the Big Bang isn't an event *in* the universe; it's the beginning of the whole stage—space and time together. Asking *where* the Big Bang happened is like asking *where* time started. Time doesn't start *in* a place; places only exist once time and space exist. The beginning is purely a temporal boundary, not a spatial point. It's not that the start had no location; it's that the very concept of "location" didn't exist before the start. The Big Bang is the origin of location itself. ### An analogy: the North Pole of the Earth Think of the Earth's surface. All lines of longitude converge at the North Pole. You could say the North Pole is the "starting point" of all northward directions—if you go north from anywhere, you end up there. But now ask: *where* on the Earth's surface is the start of north? There's no spot you can point to where "north" begins, because every place has a northward direction, and the North Pole itself doesn't have a longitude. The North Pole is not "a place where north starts"; it's the boundary condition of the coordinate system. It has a location, but it's not a "where" in the same sense as other places—you can't go north from the North Pole; there is no "north of the North Pole." The Big Bang is similar for time. It's the boundary of time, not a place in space. Just as the North Pole isn't a point you can reach and then keep walking north, the Big Bang isn't a location you can point to and call the centre. It's the moment when the entire map of spacetime came into existence. ### The "where" is, paradoxically, everywhere Now, connect this to what we actually see. The afterglow of the Big Bang (the CMB) comes from all directions equally. When we run the expansion backwards, we don't find that all matter was concentrated at a single spatial point; we find that the whole universe was in a hot, dense state everywhere, all at once, with no boundary. The "starting point" of the universe isn't somewhere in the middle of today's night sky; it's the entire sky, at the earliest moments of time. So the Big Bang had a *when*—13.8 billion years ago—but its "where" is literally *everywhere at that moment.* ### Your intuition about starts is still valid You're absolutely right that every natural process we observe has a start. The difference is that all those processes (ripples, storms, explosions) are embedded in an already-existing space and time. The Big Bang is the origin of space and time themselves. It's a category error to then demand that the origin of space and time obey the rules of things inside space and time. That doesn't make the Big Bang any less of a start; it just turns it into a different *kind* of start, one that our brains, which evolved to track lions and throw spears, were never designed to picture easily. I fully acknowledge this still sounds weird. It did to Einstein too, initially. But the fact that you're wrestling with it and refusing to accept a shallow answer shows you're not dumb at all—you're doing exactly what a good scientist does: demanding a coherent explanation for something that conflicts with deep intuition.
